  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nTemperatures will remain near or slightly above normal through the \nweekend with north to east winds generally below 15 knots. Moisture \nwill begin an increase tonight under the influence of a surface/mid-\nlevel trough. This will lead to a low (20-40%) chance of showers \nalong the coast tonight. As the trough shifts further west Wednesday \ninto early Thursday , so will the rain chances across the southern \nCoastal Plains and southern Brush Country. Beyond Thursday, rain \nchances will diminish to nearly zero. The next chances for rain will \nbe early next week as another trough approaches the area.\n\nA persistent northeast to easterly flow will continue through \nThursday/Thursday night. While the winds are expected to remain \nbetween 5-15 knots, the persistent northeast to easterly wind \ndirection and swell periods around 8 seconds could lead to minor \ncoastal flooding during the times of high tide. Guidance indicates \nand upward trend in tidal levels; however, confidence remains low to \nmedium. We will continue to monitor for possible Coastal Flood \nAdvisories. \n\nThough winds will be northeast/east through Thursday night, 8 second \nswell periods could help to increase the rip current threat, \nparticularly near piers and jetties. Will continue with a moderate \nrip current risk today and Wednesday due to swell periods around 8 \nseconds. The northeast flow tends to lower the rip current risk, but \n8 second period swells tends to increase the risk.",
